A||||63||fierosound||the REAR WING - looks only??||||1||
Z||000000||fierosound||07-19-2001||07:51 PM||||A few people have stated the Fiero rear wing is "looks only, doesn't do anything" <BR>- basically a factory "ricer" add-on.<P>NOT CORRECT!!  The  rear wing is part of the aero-package developed for the INDY Pace Car.  <BR>This consisted of the "aero" nose, ground effects, rear facia and rear wing.<P>The original Fiero coupe with 14-inch wheels had a Cd (co-efficient of drag) of 0.406  <BR>Adding the aero nose drops that to 0.372  <BR>Adding the ground effects and rear wing drops it to 0.350  <BR>In fastback GT trim is is as low as 0.340  <BR>The rear wing produces up to 250 lbs of rear downforce (varies with speed).  <BR>I believe 80-100 lbs is produced at 60 mph (looking for verification on this). <BR>The "aero" package also reduces front-end lift by 15%.<P>These are wind-tunnel proven numbers...<BR>I guess there WAS a reason the WING was on the Pace Car...<P>References:<BR>FIERO - by Gary Witzenburg (page 71)<BR>Publisher - Motorbooks International<P>Pontiac FIERO 1984-1988 (pages 40, 66)<BR>Publisher - Motorbooks International<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||firstfiero||07-19-2001||08:03 PM||||   You my friend are CORRECT!  I believe the writer of the article even said that it was a noticeable difference in the handeling.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||Fformula88||07-19-2001||08:51 PM||||I am glad you dug up the info out of the books fierosound.  I was thinking of doing the same, but was just too lazy.<P>At track speeds, that kind of downforce would be noticeable in handling.<P>Also, we shouldn't be too quick to judge all people add on wings either (just most of them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  hehe).  Some of those do create considerable downforce.  That doesn't mean the people putting them on their cars know or care about this, but I am sure some do.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||07-19-2001||09:11 PM||||I was always under the impression that due to the shape of the notchback, it creates a still space that covers almost the entire decklid. (The Fiero doesn't have a wing, it's a spoiler. Almost the same effect, but air moves differently around it, to my understanding.) Ergo, not much air that isn't already turbulent actually goes over the spoiler to make a difference... <BR>However, I bow to the windtunnel proof.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000004||Tigger||07-19-2001||09:31 PM||||A rear spoiler technically is defined as being continuous, with no gaps between itself and the car’s bodywork.  The gap in the Fiero wing is so small that it essentially makes the wing a spoiler.<P>A spoiler, as it’s name suggests, spoils the fast, smooth, low drag airflow that is, unfortunately the cause of positive lift.  <P>The spoiler reduces the local airflow velocity over the rear surface, which in turn causes a rise in pressure over this area.  Fitting a rear spoiler does not necessarily increase drag, in some cases it can reduce it.  Of course, if you eventually increase the height of the spoiler and its angle, it will eventually lower top speeds.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000005||DecadenceR||07-19-2001||09:35 PM||||I definitely noticed a handling difference when I went from my old 84 decklid to the decklid from an 86 with a spoiler. And it looks SO much better on notchbacks.||||reg||1
