Z||000023||California Kid||07-20-2001||08:15 PM||||To the one's who believe this part is a "Looks Only" item, They should carefully read and comprehend the drag coefficient numbers in the first post.<P>At the time the Fiero was developed, fuel economy was at the top of the list related to body styling (10ths of a mile per gallon were examined). The wing was developed in the wind tunnel to accomplish improved fuel economy, high speed handling, and lastly styling. GM already did all the work for you guys to get the best benefits and styling to. If you alter the position of the wing (ie raising it), you will have negative results, might look good buts that's all.<P>Did you ever wonder why some of the auto makers are putting wings on 4 door sedans ?? The primary reason is that they increase fuel economy!!!! These parts aren't pink flamingo's, unless they're modified! Aftermarket Wings are another matter, unless you're just going for looks, you'd better ask for reports on how it affects the car aero dynamics.||||reg||14
Z||000024||rogergarrison||07-20-2001||08:39 PM||||Dead wrong......the only reason you see them on most new cars to increase the price. Do you really think that $5.00 worth of plastic should sell for $500-$700. There just soaking you for more money so you can look like your Geo is a German sports car. If they did increase anything else then why are they only an option, you dont have to get them on most cars. I know this because I install this 'option' on 3 dealerships cars for them. BTW I get $250 more for myself to paint and put them on.||||reg||1
Z||000025||MrPBody||07-20-2001||10:34 PM||||Re: tuft testing --<P>I tied strips of surveyor's tape to my luggage rack, and drove out Stone Mountain Freeway at ~80 mph.  (If the cops read this, I'm lying.)  At all highway speeds,  air over most of the rear deck flows <I>forward</I>, not back.  Only at the edges does air flow back.  Apparently, there is no rearward airflow over most of the wing; therefore, the wing is for looks only.<P>But it does look good!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||California Kid||07-20-2001||11:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>Dead wrong......the only reason you see them on most new cars to increase the price. Do you really think that $5.00 worth of plastic should sell for $500-$700. There just soaking you for more money so you can look like your Geo is a German sports car. If they did increase anything else then why are they only an option, you dont have to get them on most cars. I know this because I install this 'option' on 3 dealerships cars for them. BTW I get $250 more for myself to paint and put them on.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First of all you don't any knowledge of what the OEM's pay for the part/s, assembly plant costs to incorporate it into the line. Sure the price is marked up, that's where the OEM's make money, they don't make it on the no options base car.<P>In nearly all cases the spoiler is an option for the above reason and also that they don't want to increase the base price to cover the cost of the spoiler (because car pricing is very competative), so they make the customer absorb the cost if they want it. Most OEM's will only make the spoiler standard equipment on their "high volume" models where they need to get the best government CAFE fuel mileage results to improve there overall average for all the vehicles they produce. Nearly all vehicles priced above the mid range average have either a spoiler or raised feature in the deck lid to improve air flow over the rear of the vehicle to lower the drag coeffient for fuel economy and performance.<P>There are some models that have spoilers that don't do a damn thing (may even make the car worse in performance) but make the car look like it's supposed to be fast and these are typically what are called entry level cars.<P>You can't see the results of the rear spoiler on your Fiero by taping yarn strips or thread on the rear spoiler. The only way to really see what's happening is in a wind tunnel with smoke injected into the air stream in front of the car. The area of improvement with the spoiler is the entire zone from the rear edge of the roof rearward, not just at the spoiler.<P>Since you "challenged me" by saying I'm dead wrong, I say you're dead wrong when it comes to the spoiler that was developed for the Fiero. The only reason I'm responding is not for your benefit, but for others who read this forum, that want to make intelligent decisions.   ||||reg||6
Z||000027||filthyscarecrow||07-20-2001||11:41 PM||||i'll have to look up the name of the book, but i was reading this book on aerodynamics and the author pointed out something about wings: the low pressure side is affected much more than the high pressure side as far as smoothness goes. the fiero wing (he used this as an example- i believe his exact words were "the wing on the fiero in my garage...") is pretty much worthless in this respect because of the two channels into which the stantions mount. even if the wing was worth something beforehand, these grooves affect the underside- the low pressure side- disrupting whatever flow it actually encounters. it apparantly (if i understnad this right) disrupts the laminar flow under the wing, making it turbulent and increasing pressure on that side of the wing. er something like that. remember bernoulli's principle: faster fluid=lower pressure. turbulent air moves slower. high pressure on one side of a wing + high pressure on the other side of a wing = decorative trim. i dunno what go-tard told you that it makes up to 250 lbs of download, but they should stick a tennis ball in their mouth to prevent further embarassment. as a spoiler it might be effective though. from what i understand, spoilers aren't actually there to produce downforce themselves, they simply trip up the air coming off the back of the car, filling in the vacuum behind the car more efficiently and improving flow around the car. NASCAR's spoilers are essentially gurney flaps, making the entire car the wing. because they're not very big, the car cannot be a very high-lift wing, but because the air over the car is being tripped up and slowed down, and the air under the car is travelling opposite the car at the same speed, the air over the car has higher pressure, pushing the car down (nothing really sucks, everything pushes from high to low pressure). <BR>the other statement you made that makes NO sense is that the coefficient of drag with the wing is lower than without the wing. i don't know why you didn't question this. DOWNLOAD=DRAG! plain and simple, there is no free lunch on this one. if that thing is making 250lbs of download, it will inherently create an arseload of drag too. your terms contradict one another. if our wings make downforce while reducing drag, don't you think you'd see them on F1 cars and indy cars? this is a prime example of why you can't believe everything manufacturers tell you. they WANT you to buy their stuff, so they're gonna make it sound as cool as possible. don't just buy into it, question WHY you should buy into it. if you don't, you're just one of the sheep. <BR>the wing is a decoration, plain and simple, just like others have said. the problem is that for a decoration, it is not only very heavy (i dont know how much it weighs, but i'd put it at at least 20lbs. maybe they were talking gravitational downforce hehe...), but it is also at the very end of the car where you don't want excess weight. the car handles better with it's weight concentrated in the center and as low as it can get. why would you put 20 lbs at a point as far away from that ideal spot that it can get? it defeats the purpose of trying to make the car handle. thus (dont hate me) it is rice. that's what we make fun of ricers for- putting on stuff (and heavy stuff at that) that doesn't do anything. it's yuor definition, not theirs, adn by your definition, our wings qualify as rice. i'm not saying they dno't look good though...<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000028||California Kid||07-21-2001||12:09 AM||||Some people just can't seem to understand that you can't learn or understand everything from reading books, especially on aero dynamics !!!  Why the heck do you think they use Wind Tunnels, it's because theory is just that, theory !!, It doesn't mean it will come out the same way in the real world, that's why it's tested and developed. Your entitled to your opinion of course, but before anyone calls the next person wrong, they should support it with crediable proof and test results.<P>Of course that's just my opinion, I could be wrong. ||||reg||4
Z||000029||rogergarrison||07-21-2001||12:33 AM||||youve seen some of your 'real world' tests here. Not one thing substantiates your idea. Its all marketing. The manufacturers push the idea that your car would look way cooler with this little thing on it. More people buy it, like he said above like sheep. Sooner or later they figure....hey lets put it on everycar we can and well make a few million dollars more for our stockholders. Thats what happened with things like air conditioning, power windows. There not necessity. everyone kept buying and buying them till they said if we make AC standard we make another $1,000 per car. before the eighties there were more made without them. Now I dont think you can even buy a car without AC. Pretty sure youd stump the salesman trying to delete it. And AC is the one biggest threat to gas economy so go figure, since you say everyone is trying to strive for max fuel milage. I raced stock cars for years (with the barn door on trunklid) and flown for 40 years so I think Im a little bit knowlegable in the area. I still say same thing.......looks........and your dead wrong thinking otherwise.<P>last comment, i promise  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
