Z||000031||b lo 0||07-21-2001||01:04 AM||||That whole "not in the airflow thing, that's why they make 7" wing risers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  and Ken, take it easy, bubba.  It seems you're out to offend somebody, and it's probably gonna happen real soon (we all know how touchy some people on the forum can get-myself included)<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">      <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000032||filthyscarecrow||07-21-2001||01:43 AM||||dude, cali kid, the mistake yuo made is that you assumed that you can do the testing without reading the book first. how will you know what you're looking at if you don't know how it's supposed to work. you never mentioned anything about principles of fluid dynamics in your argument, do you understnad them? if not, how do you expect to understand what the wing is doing when you tape your little pieces of yarn to it. the reason books and papers exist is so that we can use a general guideline to determine WHETHER to test in the first place. if you read the books, you'll understand that there's no point in testing the wing because based on principles we already know, the wing is a decoration. why waste the time and money testing to find out something we can figure out using common sense and a couple books? eh, i guess it's your time and money... let me know how it turns out.<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...<P>and i really want to meet the diver that can feel the difference beteween a car with a decorative wing and one without it at 65mph. methinks your ass-o-meter needs calibration...<p>[This message has been edited by filthyscarecrow (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||Tigger||07-21-2001||02:21 AM||||No reason to test the wing?  I'm right, your wrong, it's for decoration only, case closed... huh?<P>Fluid dynamics isn't a common sense science...  Things can happen in fluid flows that may defy everything we would expect using common sense and/or high level mathematics.<P>Flow visualization using the wool tuff, oil streaks, water/poster paint powder, titanium dioxide, and ultra-violet flourecent dyes on the surface, can be very useful in understanding airflows.<P>The only disadvantage of these methods is they only show what is going on with the boundary levels of airflow <I>near</I> the surface.<P>We can all tape tuffs of yarn to our decklids and make our own conclusions.  The precise way to tell what's going on with the airflow is to use the smoke plume method in a wind tunnel, or try to predict the flows using CFD.  Only then will we be able to see what's going on with the airflow <I>above</I> the surface.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||rodmcneill||07-21-2001||03:12 AM||||Damn, and I thought the purpose of the spoiler was to help obscure rear window vision!||||reg||12
Z||000035||Pontiaddict||07-21-2001||03:56 AM||||I'll agree that about 80% of the rear spoiler doesn't see fast moving air.<BR>So what about the parts that do?<BR>Fiero's don't need rear downforce anyway, since adding downforce takes weight off the already light (and at speed it's getting lighter due to lift) front end.<BR>It's there as a spoiler (I'll add "in my belief." as a sort of disclaimer)<BR>Notice how the 89 Fiero prototypes have a blocky stuck up rear end compared to earlier models. GM found out that sticking the car's butt up in the air helps with high speed stability. Look at a new Corvette as an example. Think of the spoiler as being a crutch to accomplish this without a complete redesign. Then again they did make the back of the Fastback GT's more squared off.<P>One other thing, A lot of air does hit the sides of the spoiler. Why not think about the edges with their inverted winglets as having any aerodynamic importance instead of the big flat part that's mainly in contact with dead air? (Yes this does kind of sound like I'm contradicting myself, but I am interested in what you guys think of that part of the wing.)<P>--------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><FONT SIZE="1">I hope I didn't just make a fool out of myself, not that it would be the first time or anything</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
