Z||000045||firstfiero||07-22-2001||11:04 AM||||    All I know is knotchbacks look like ****  without them...and I don't want to even discuss how nasty the luggage rack looks!<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000046||MrPBody||07-22-2001||12:53 PM||||Argue with GM?  Sure, why not?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'm not saying that GM lies outright, but . . . .<P>The <I>combined</I> effect of the rear wing and the aero package reduced drag coefficient from .377 to .350.  (Compared, I suppose, to the square coupe nose.)  I'm guessing that improvement is mostly due to the aero nose, and the wing does zilch or nearly so.<P>Not trying to start -- or inflame -- an argument; however, as far as I can tell there ain't enough airflow over the wing to have much effect.  The wing is too small, and airflow is too slow, turbulent, and in the wrong direction.  Presumably, that's why IMSA Fieros use tray or whale-tail spoilers -- they scoop air from the sides to make high pressure over the deck lid.<P>As cousin Johnny might say: "No air on the wing, it don't do a thing."  <I>Sorry, that's bad.</I><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000047||California Kid||07-22-2001||01:51 PM||||Seems quite a few of you are missing the whole design intention of the wing. The primary purpose is to smooth out the air flow rearward of the roof area. Without getting into a bunch of technical jargon, it basically sets up a high pressure zone in the forward deck lid area which doesn't allow the air coming off the roof to dive down onto the deck lid. Wind Tunnel photo shows this was accomplished with the IMSA spoiler, spoiler developed by GM also improved air flow, but not to pure racing standards (that's why GM got IMSA involved in improving the designs for racing). GM knew the 84 design had aero flaws mainly at the front (that's why they went to newer front facia), and at the rear (that's what drove the rearend design changes and addition of the wing). Article clip from 84 Road and Track below:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Clip_of_RT_Fiero_Article_84.gif"> <BR>While the Wind Tunnel photo only shows one position off the smoke wand, it clearly shows that the air stream doesn't dive down and turbulate on the deck lid, as it would without the spoiler (tried to find some pictures, but guess our cars are getting too old for people to keep records available for them on the net).<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/IMSA2Small.jpg"> <BR>Just because you can't see smoke hitting the wing doesn't mean it's doing nothing. The development of all the Fiero Aero Improvements, Factory and IMSA Parts was a costly development exercise. No one in their right mind would do all this research and development to put a "looks only part" on their vehicle.<P>I know I wasn't going to post in this string again, sorry !!! Just don't want readers to think they have a useless piece of added weight on their decklid.||||reg||6
Z||000048||ray b||07-22-2001||02:33 PM||||chapprel[sp] jim halls cars used a high wing<BR>that could be moved. angle of attack changed on the fly. banned in racing but still works best to increase downforce in corners and reduce it on straits. works best if connected to sept. not bodywork. any one tryed it on the fiero???<BR>point two gt front end caused headlite door lift on my car 87 se airo that my 88 coupe and formula don't do, why???<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000049||California Kid||07-22-2001||03:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>chapprel[sp] <BR>gt front end caused headlite door lift on my car 87 se airo that my 88 coupe and formula don't do, why???<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Has to do with air pressure under the headlamp door, once it lifts the high velocity air flow over the nose pushes it up, quit severely the faster you go. There has to be some difference in the air flow between your cars if they don't react the same way (different facia, chin spoiler, curtain flaps behind facia, etc.)<P>Both doors on my '88 GT start to open at 105 mph, when you push past 105 they bag up and down. This will destroy your headlamp doors (frame not, not the top cover), someone makes a retaining wire (Rodney Dickman ??)to prevent this from happening. Obviously GM wasn't worried about this due to the speed it happens at.<P>As far as an adjustable rear wing, nobody that I know of has taken the time and money to develope for the Fiero. IMSA developed within car setup rules. It would look pretty trick but seriouly doubt it would help out that much unless you were going to run at LeMans or Indy.   ||||reg||1
Z||000050||MrPBody||07-22-2001||04:52 PM||||Call me a hard-headed skeptic if you will . . .<P>IMSA spoilers -- with side fences to catch rear-flowing air -- do generate downforce.  As the photo suggests, an IMSA spoiler also diverts air upward, evidently reducing turbulence over the car's rear.<P>Does the production wing have a similar effect?  I doubt it.  The stock wing sits above the deck, so air can flow under it; I don't think it acts as an effective spoiler.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000051||California Kid||07-22-2001||06:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>Does the production wing have a similar effect?  I doubt it.  The stock wing sits above the deck, so air can flow under it; I don't think it acts as an effective spoiler.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The design is tough to explain without some photo's to show you all the airflow around the back of the car. The rear facia/tailamp area is a negative pressure zone that literally sucks the air up from under the car, and wants to send it back up onto the decklid. The air gap between the spoiler collects this forward moving air where it is met by air coming from the front, and basically they cancel each other out (meaning the underside of the spoiler is a neutral zone, no flow is intended there). In effect GM was able to get very good effects with this design, make it a bolt on, and not change the design of the deck lid (as it was used on all models). The whole principle was to clean up what I'll call "tirty air turbulance" coming from the underside of vehicle, sides, and top. You can't see these air flow patterns, but they do create resistance. The spoiler just helps prevent the air stream from getting all screwed up at the rear of the car. <P>The IMSA designs had more latitude because they were add ons, and didn't impact GM's production molds, production process, paint shop, etc. Don't know what their down force numbers were, but the main goal was to make the car as "slippery" as possible for racing.<P>I'm not going to get into the "down force" area, I believe the posted numbers, just don't want to get into all the dynamics that make it happen. The IMSA spoiler used on the race cars was aluminum and had what they call a "wicker strip" across the top rear edge (same as Indy Car and Formula One) of the spoiler. They would change the "strip" to a taller one if they were running a track that required more down force on the rear end (typically high speed turns where you needed more weight on the rear tires to prevent lateral G's from pushing your ass end out). <P>||||reg||1
Z||000052||8Ball||07-22-2001||07:47 PM||||One definate example (believe it or not) of a wing helping a car is the 96 ford tarus.<BR>We rented one that did not have that tiny lip on the trailing edge of the trunk and at 60 the rear end swayed a lil. then we drove one that had the tiny wing and no sway!! Hard to imagine that something so small could make that big a differance||||reg||1
Z||000053||California Kid||07-22-2001||08:41 PM||||Hey 8ball.....glad you posted a note on the Taurus.  Other examples are the Pontiac Bonneville (small wing), Olds Aurora/Buick Riveria (decklid is curved up slightly at rear). When they went to the old "Boat Tail" look, air flows got real screwed up, resulting in a slight rear end "buffeting" you could feel in the seat of your pants. Annoyed test track drivers of prototypes so much, they put them in the wind tunnel to find a fix. A little can go a long way if you put it in the right place !!!!||||reg||6
Z||000054||MrPBody||07-22-2001||11:56 PM||||Let us agree to disagree.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000055||Ken Wittlief||07-23-2001||09:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>"THe optional rear deck spoiler, along with the aerodynamic package, not only help give the car the look of a performance machine, but reduce the Fiero's already impressive .377 drag coefficient to a drago coefficent of .350"<P>That is quoted from the 85 Do It Yourself service manual.Anyone wanna argue with GM?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> there is nothing to argue with - they stated it plainly<P> the rear spoiler gave it the LOOKS of a performance car - and the "aerodynamic package" lowered the drag cooefficent.<P>  if the SPOILER lowered the DC it would be PART of the aerodynamics package - thats what areodynamics is - the DC!<P>  come on guys - give it up - you cant deflect the air UP to increase downward force on the rear tires, AND reduce drag at the same time.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000056||Ken Wittlief||07-23-2001||10:25 AM||||This whole thread is getting to be embarassing - Im glad its not in the technical section!<P>1. Gm did all the wind tunnel testing with the wing and the ground effects stuff for the RACE car version of the fiero - the car was really raced - but there was almost nothing in the car that was used in production.<P>2. the reason car manufactures race their production cars in modified versions is to sell the production cars - so they try to make the production cars look like the race versions - LOOK LIKE!<P>3. when the new VW turbo beetle came out it had a rear spoiler that RETRACTS! it only comes out when the car is going above 90MPH. below that is doesnt help handling enought to warrent the LOSS in mpg it introduces. But of COURSE they had to put an OVERRIDE switch on the dash so we can force the spoiler to STAY OUT all the time - so we can look cool at 10mph in the Kmart parking lot. I dont know if this all made it into the production car, but that is the way the engineers planned it.<P>4. the most aerodynamic shape for a car is something close to an egg, with the pointing part towards the rear. that would give you the lowest Cx for the volume of space enclosed - the faster you want to go the longer the egg shape is drawn out.<P>  but since you want the car to stick to the ground at speed you also need to use ground effects - this is why cars in the 90's all became 'bubble cars' and they all started to look the same - and this is why they all have the spoilers UNDER the front bumper which are NOT an option cause they really do improve the Cx of the car. So you cant opt to NOT have one.<P>5. to get the lowest Cx on a car you have to keep the airflow LAMINAR over the whole surface of it - you are back to the egg shape. the turbulance that happens behind the rear window and the rear of the Fiero cant be 'fixed' by deflecting the air upwards. If anything you would want to deflect the air down around the rear of the car - which would have a negative effect on handling - it would provide lift on the rear wheels. A spoiler might be tuned to find a sweet spot to minimize the turbulance at certain speeds but the air behind the window and the tail lights is still turbulant.<P>6. the reason car manufactures put their cars in wind tunnels is NOT cause the math doesnt work and this is all an art - the reason is cause the perfect egg shape of the car must be altered to make the car more useful for passengers and trunk space, and to house the drive train - [the new VW beetle is pretty close to the egg shape though] <P>  thats why they put the car in the wind tunnel - to see how much they have screwed up the shape of it, and to see if there are any obvious improvements that can be made - but to be perfectly honest - Im not sure wind tunnel testing is used on production cars at all anymore - computer aided design has gotten to the point where its really not necessary.<P> some people have taken the time and effort to try to explain - below 90 to 100mph the spoilers on production cars are for looks only - above that they are only marginally helpful - if you have the need to corner at 120 mph where you are reaching the .8G lateral acceleration limits of the Fiero handling abilities, and you need to push the envelope a  few hundreds of a G higher, then I cant imagine who you are or where you are driving your car like this - I hope there is a crash truck and rescue vehicles nearby when you find out what the handling limits are at 120mph.<P>  there is nothing wrong with putting or having a cool looking paint job, stripes, moulding, stickers, lights, trim or wings on your car if you like the way it looks. But if you drive around at 75mph thinking all this stuff is doing anything to make your car perform better - then you are kidding yourself - and anyone who knows how this stuff works will be laughing at you behind your back.<P>  Personally I was planning on taking the luggage rack lid off my '85SE and getting the formula style deck lid with wing - simply because I like the way they look - but after all this nonsense I think I will go the other way and see if I can make my car smoother than it is - and wingless.<P>  there is a different kind of beauty in a machine that had engineering integrity in its design.||||reg||13
Z||000057||b lo 0||07-23-2001||12:56 PM||||ummmmm... yall seem to be missing how the IMSA SPOILER works.  See, wings ans spoilers create downforce differently.  A wing creates downforce right there, at the wing, requiring stands that can support the downforce.  Spoilers, however, do NOT make downforce, persay, but in reality the flow over the spoiler creates a low pressure zone directly behind the car, thus SUCKING air out from underneath the car, and creating a vacuum.  That's how Pro Stock drag spoilers and Winston Cup cars create downforce.  Ever watched Nascar and see a guy get squirrely when another car gets close to his rear bumper?  The air dam on the following car destroys the low pressure zone.  See, there are advantages to learning aerodynamics when you train to be a collision repair specialist  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  and Ken, not to flame, but you just HAVE to be right, don't you?||||reg||1
Z||000058||FieroMike||07-23-2001||01:40 PM||||Well, I don't know if any engineers have replied... but the picture clearly shows that there is a downforce created by the wing.  Otherwise the smoke would DROP down to the wing because there would be lower air pressure above the wing.  There's not though, there is High Pressure above the wing keeping the smoke away. (Hence less pressure under the wing = downforce)  The pressure in my opinion is not created from air moving directly over the top of the car.  Rather the air on the sides follow the line of the notchback (which slightly aim in towards the center of the car) and this is where the air is being manipulated.  I don't think that it is making a huge difference, or would be effective at normal driving speeds.  In race applications and people carving the lanes at 90+ I could see that it would help a little.  The engine is in the rear and the rear won't really be lifting anyway, it's the front that needs to stay down.<P>-Mike<P>Just my .02!||||reg||4
Z||000059||Ken Wittlief||07-23-2001||04:34 PM||||Im not sure what it means to 'be right'.<P>reality exists independantly of us. We form a model of reality in our minds, that we use to interact and predict what the real world will do.<P>the more accurate your mental model, the better you understand the real world, the more successful you will be in all your endevours - you will focus your energy and resources on things that will take you closer to where you want to be<P>and you wont waste your time and energy on stuff that doesnt function the way you need it too.<P> a factory spoiler was an option on the last two cars I bought, Im pretty sure it was $1,000 (i will check in a second).<P>  how much would it have to improve the mileage of the vehicles to pay for itself over the life of the car - if it really does improve the Cx of the car. The second last car gets 45mpg at 70mph, the last car I bought new gets 35mph highway.<P>  if the wing costs $1k, and the car lasts 150,000 miles, at 45mpg it will use 3,333 gallons of fuel. at $1.80 / gallon thats $6,000 in fuel costs.<P>  so it would have to drop $1000 off the fuel =  555 gallons less =&gt; 2778 gallon to go 150,000 miles =&gt; 54 mpg.<P>  so the wing on the back of a saturn SL would have to increase the mpg rating from 45mpg to 54mpg to pay for itself at $1000 for the option.<P>  anybody wanna bet it really gives a 20% boost to mileage economy?!<P>  there is only one correct answer to whether a wing is functional or not. Its not a matter of who is being right, its a question of physics and economics. <P>   I dont thing its a bad character trait to strive to understand things correctly, to debate the facts and issues, or to point out to someone else that the placebo the doctor gave them is a sugar pill.<P>edit: Okay I checked the saturn web site - the rear spoiler is $225 to $250 depending on which car you stick it on - so it would only have to increase the mpgs of the car by 5% instead of 20%.<P>  but even that - if that extra piece of metal could improve the mpg rating from 45 to 47.3mpg, im CERTAIN it would be standard -and they would be touting its glory at the dealership when you buy the car.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000060||StuGood||07-23-2001||05:34 PM||||Man!  I <B>have</B> been involved in aircraft configuration design, but I probably shouldn't touch this subject with a ten-foot wing stanchion! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Okay - I'm just a simple-minded engineer, but here's my thinking:<BR>1) The Fiero wing <B>does</B> produce a certain amount of downforce, even at rest.  How much?  Weigh the wing and you'll know!  <P>2) If I wanted to know how much (aero) downforce the Fiero wing produces, I'd mount it to the car on with little spring mounts (like, Belleville washers?), and mount some displacement transducers (like LVDTs, etc.) under the trunk lid.  Record displacement versus airspeed, and we'd know something.  A different mounting setup, and we could get drag numbers, too - but only for the wing itself, which isn't what I'm interested in.  I would want to know what the effect on the car+ wing is. <P>If I wanted to know the effects on overall drag (which sounds like an OshKosh fitment issue  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), I'd want more instrumentation, along with a full size wind tunnel.  Like Kansas!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Seriously, while you may be able to make some judgements about what's going on, from smoke, strips of paper or yarn, from my (very limited) experience, it's often the interaction between aero surfaces (such as wing to body or engine pylon to wing) that is hardest to characterize and predict, and it can have a big effect on performance.  That's why I leave it to the aero-heads  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000061||California Kid||07-23-2001||05:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>  there is nothing to argue with - they stated it plainly<P> the rear spoiler gave it the LOOKS of a performance car - and the "aerodynamic package" lowered the drag cooefficent.<P>  if the SPOILER lowered the DC it would be PART of the aerodynamics package - thats what areodynamics is - the DC!<P>  come on guys - give it up - you cant deflect the air UP to increase downward force on the rear tires, AND reduce drag at the same time.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Very nice twist on how you interupt the post by Galen quoted by GM. Especially liked the way you changed it to suit your purpose. Certainly isn't the way I understand the sentence to read. GM knew that if you wanted to get the full effect of the areo package, you had to shell out money for the optional wing to get the lower CD, that's why they offered the wing as a seperate option.<P>It would be quite funny to hear some GM Tech guy responding to the question "What about this wing on the back, what's it there for?" and the Tech guy saying "It's just to make the car look racey, doesn't do a damn thing but add weight, and were going to charge the customer $400 bucks for it! The Executives told us to come up with something that looks cool, make it look like it works, then they'll have to have it, and we make big money!"<P>Everybody is entitled to their own thoughts and make their own decisions. Some people will take their wing off, hang it on the wall, and put books on it. Some will leave it on because it looks good. Some will think a lot of development went into wing and be proud to have it on their car.<P>You made some good points in your other post, some I don't agree with, and I don't think I'm going to see a true shaped egg car in my lifetime (the bettle is only half an egg). By the way "Wind Tunnels" are still actively being used, Race Teams are consulted "up front" when a concept vehicle is still in clay mold, they even put the clay mold in a wind tunnel.<P>Personally, I don't have it on my show car, not because I feel it does nothing, just that I liked the looks of the IMSA Whale Tail and know it functions very well if I chose to get alittle crazy with my car.<P>On a positive note: This string really made some people think.<P>Aside from all the above, I'm feeling the same way you are about this string. Flames or no flames, this is it for me.<BR> <BR>Of course that's just my opinion, I might be wrong.||||reg||6
Z||000062||Ken Wittlief||07-24-2001||11:09 AM||||<B>"overall drag"</B><P>  is this when you go out for the evening<P> wearing your sisters farmer-john jeans?!<P>  does it matter if she bought then in the girls or mens section of Sears and Sawbucks?!||||reg||1
Z||000063||rogergarrison||07-24-2001||11:26 AM||||what all this boils down to guys, put it on or take it off, whichever you want. Just dont expect something totally remarkable to happen either way. As for anything potentially gained in downforce, wouldnt the extra weight of hauling it around negate the benefit anyway? They say it decreases your gas milage just hauling around crap in your trunk. but then you could counter that by putting 60# of air in the tires.......but then......... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
